  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1=$",
  "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-7$ into the right-hand-side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-14\\pi )+1=-10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})+1=2\\sqrt{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
